<!-- ========== WHAT IS CHINESE TEA ========== -->
<section class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-4 py-20">
  <div class="max-w-3xl">
    <p class="text-leaf-600 text-sm font-mono uppercase tracking-[0.18em] mb-3">The Essentials</p>
    <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight text-zinc-900 mb-6">
      What Is Chinese Tea?
    </h2>
    <p class="text-lg text-zinc-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">
      Chinese tea is more than a beverage — it's a 4,000-year-old tradition woven into daily life, art, philosophy, and medicine. Unlike the tea bags found in most Western supermarkets, authentic Chinese tea is made from whole leaves of the <em>Camellia sinensis</em> plant, processed using methods refined over centuries.
    </p>
    <p class="text-lg text-zinc-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">
      What makes Chinese tea unique is its <strong>staggering variety</strong>: six major categories based on oxidation level and processing — from fresh, unoxidized green tea to aged, fermented pu-erh. Each category contains hundreds of named varieties tied to specific mountains, villages, and terroirs.
      <span class="block mt-3 text-base">The earliest written record comes from <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Classic_of_Tea" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-leaf-600 underline hover:text-leaf-700">Lu Yu’s <em>The Classic of Tea</em> (茶经, 760 CE)</a>, which elevated tea from medicine to cultural art form. What began on the misty slopes of <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_tea" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-leaf-600 underline hover:text-leaf-700">Yunnan</a> — the botanical birthplace of Camellia sinensis — spread across China and eventually the world.</span>
    </p>
    <p class="text-lg text-zinc-600 leading-relaxed">
      Whether you're looking for a daily morning cup, a meditative brewing ritual, or a tea to age like fine wine, Chinese tea has something for every palate.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ========== SIX TYPES OF CHINESE TEA ========== -->
<section id="types" class="bg-bone-50 py-20">
  <div class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-4">
    <div class="text-center mb-14">
      <p class="text-leaf-600 text-sm font-mono uppercase tracking-[0.18em] mb-3">The Foundation</p>
      <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight text-zinc-900 mb-4">
        The Six Types of Chinese Tea
      </h2>
      <p class="text-zinc-500 max-w-2xl mx-auto">
        Chinese tea is classified by oxidation level and processing method. Each type has a distinct flavor profile, brewing temperature, and character.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3 gap-6" id="tea-type-cards">
      <!-- Green Tea -->
      <div class="group bg-white rounded-2xl border border-zinc-200 p-6 hover:shadow-lg hover:border-leaf-200 transition-all duration-300 cursor-pointer" data-type="green">
        <div class="w-12 h-12 rounded-xl overflow-hidden mb-4 group-hover:scale-110 transition-transform"><img src="/images/green-tea.webp" alt="Green tea leaves" class="w-full h-full object-cover" loading="lazy" width="48" height="48" /></div>
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-lg text-zinc-900 mb-1">Green Tea <span class="text-sm font-normal text-zinc-400">绿茶</span></h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-zinc-500 mb-1">Unoxidized · Fresh · Vegetal</p>
        <p class="text-xs text-zinc-400 mb-3 leading-relaxed">Leaves are <strong>pan-fired or oven-dried</strong> immediately after picking to halt oxidation, preserving their green color and fresh character. The most ancient tea processing method, dating back over 1,500 years. Best consumed within 6-12 months of harvest for peak freshness.</p>
        <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-1.5">
          <span class="px-2.5 py-0.5 text-xs rounded-full bg-emerald-50 text-emerald-700">Longjing</span>
          <span class="px-2.5 py-0.5 text-xs rounded-full bg-emerald-50 text-emerald-700">Biluochun</span>
          <span class="px-2.5 py-0.5 text-xs rounded-full bg-emerald-50 text-emerald-700">Maofeng</span>
        </div>
        <p class="text-xs text-zinc-400 mt-3">Brew: 160-175°F · 2-3 min</p>
      </div>

      <!-- White Tea -->
      <div class="group bg-white rounded-2xl border border-zinc-200 p-6 hover:shadow-lg hover:border-leaf-200 transition-all duration-300 cursor-pointer" data-type="white">
        <div class="w-12 h-12 rounded-xl overflow-hidden mb-4 group-hover:scale-110 transition-transform"><img src="/images/white-tea.webp" alt="White tea buds" class="w-full h-full object-cover" loading="lazy" width="48" height="48" /></div>
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-lg text-zinc-900 mb-1">White Tea <span class="text-sm font-normal text-zinc-400">白茶</span></h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-zinc-500 mb-1">Minimal Processing · Delicate · Sweet</p>
        <p class="text-xs text-zinc-400 mb-3 leading-relaxed">The <strong>least processed of all teas</strong> — white tea is simply withered and dried, with no rolling, pan-firing, or oxidation. This minimal intervention preserves the leaf's natural sweetness and delicate character. White tea is made from <strong>young buds and leaves</strong> covered in fine silvery down, which gives the tea its name. It's the most forgiving to brew and the gentlest on the palate — the ideal starting point for any tea journey.</p>
        <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-1.5">
          <span class="px-2.5 py-0.5 text-xs rounded-full bg-amber-50 text-amber-700">Silver Needle</span>
          <span class="px-2.5 py-0.5 text-xs rounded-full bg-amber-50 text-amber-700">White Peony</span>
          <span class="px-2.5 py-0.5 text-xs rounded-full bg-amber-50 text-amber-700">Shou Mei</span>
        </div>
        <p class="text-xs text-zinc-400 mt-3">Brew: 175-185°F · 3-5 min</p>
      </div>

      <!-- Yellow Tea -->
      <div class="group bg-white rounded-2xl border border-zinc-200 p-6 hover:shadow-lg hover:border-leaf-200 transition-all duration-300 cursor-pointer" data-type="yellow">
        <div class="w-12 h-12 rounded-xl overflow-hidden mb-4 group-hover:scale-110 transition-transform"><img src="/images/yellow-tea.webp" alt="Yellow tea leaves" class="w-full h-full object-cover" loading="lazy" width="48" height="48" /></div>
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-lg text-zinc-900 mb-1">Yellow Tea <span class="text-sm font-normal text-zinc-400">黄茶</span></h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-zinc-500 mb-1">Lightly Oxidized · Mellow · Rare</p>
        <p class="text-xs text-zinc-400 mb-3 leading-relaxed">The <strong>rarest category</strong> — only a handful of producers still make it. After pan-firing like green tea, leaves undergo <strong>men huan</strong> (sealing yellow): a gentle, multi-hour sweat that removes grassy notes and creates a uniquely mellow, almost buttery character. A true connoisseur’s find.</p>
        <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-1.5">
          <span class="px-2.5 py-0.5 text-xs rounded-full bg-yellow-50 text-yellow-700">Junshan Yinzhen</span>
          <span class="px-2.5 py-0.5 text-xs rounded-full bg-yellow-50 text-yellow-700">Mengding Huangya</span>
        </div>
        <p class="text-xs text-zinc-400 mt-3">Brew: 175-185°F · 2-3 min</p>
      </div>

      <!-- Oolong Tea -->
      <div class="group bg-white rounded-2xl border border-zinc-200 p-6 hover:shadow-lg hover:border-leaf-200 transition-all duration-300 cursor-pointer" data-type="oolong">
        <div class="w-12 h-12 rounded-xl overflow-hidden mb-4 group-hover:scale-110 transition-transform"><img src="/images/oolong-tea.webp" alt="Oolong tea leaves" class="w-full h-full object-cover" loading="lazy" width="48" height="48" /></div>
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-lg text-zinc-900 mb-1">Oolong Tea <span class="text-sm font-normal text-zinc-400">乌龙茶</span></h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-zinc-500 mb-1">Partially Oxidized · Floral to Roasted</p>
        <p class="text-xs text-zinc-400 mb-3 leading-relaxed">The <strong>most diverse category</strong> — oxidation ranges from 10% (floral Tie Guan Yin) to 70% (roasted Da Hong Pao). Leaves are bruised, rolled, and sometimes roasted. Many oolongs are <strong>tightly rolled into balls</strong> that unfurl across 5-8+ infusions, each steep revealing new layers.</p>
        <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-1.5">
          <span class="px-2.5 py-0.5 text-xs rounded-full bg-orange-50 text-orange-700">Tie Guan Yin</span>
          <span class="px-2.5 py-0.5 text-xs rounded-full bg-orange-50 text-orange-700">Da Hong Pao</span>
          <span class="px-2.5 py-0.5 text-xs rounded-full bg-orange-50 text-orange-700">Dan Cong</span>
        </div>
        <p class="text-xs text-zinc-400 mt-3">Brew: 185-205°F · 30-60s</p>
      </div>

      <!-- Black Tea -->
      <div class="group bg-white rounded-2xl border border-zinc-200 p-6 hover:shadow-lg hover:border-leaf-200 transition-all duration-300 cursor-pointer" data-type="black">
        <div class="w-12 h-12 rounded-xl overflow-hidden mb-4 group-hover:scale-110 transition-transform"><img src="/images/black-tea.webp" alt="Black tea leaves" class="w-full h-full object-cover" loading="lazy" width="48" height="48" /></div>
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-lg text-zinc-900 mb-1">Black Tea <span class="text-sm font-normal text-zinc-400">红茶</span></h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-zinc-500 mb-1">Fully Oxidized · Malty · Robust</p>
        <p class="text-xs text-zinc-400 mb-3 leading-relaxed">Called <strong>hong cha</strong> (red tea) in Chinese — named for the reddish liquor, not the leaf. The leaves are fully oxidized before drying, creating deep, malty flavors. Chinese black teas are generally <strong>smoother and less astringent</strong> than Indian varieties and are excellent without milk.</p>
        <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-1.5">
          <span class="px-2.5 py-0.5 text-xs rounded-full bg-red-50 text-red-700">Keemun</span>
