<!-- ========== WATER QUALITY GUIDE ========== -->
<section id="water" class="py-20">
  <div class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-4">
    <div class="text-center mb-14">
      <p class="text-sky-600 text-sm font-mono uppercase tracking-[0.18em] mb-3">The Foundation</p>
      <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight text-zinc-900 mb-4">
        The Right Water for Chinese Tea
      </h2>
      <p class="text-zinc-500 max-w-2xl mx-auto">
        Tea is 99% water. Lu Yu wrote in the Tea Classic that mountain spring water is best — but what does that mean for you at home?
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 max-w-5xl mx-auto">
      <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl border border-zinc-200 p-6">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-zinc-900 mb-2 flex items-center gap-2"><span class="text-sky-600">&#128167;</span> Spring Water (Bottled)</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-zinc-500 leading-relaxed"><strong>Ideal.</strong> Naturally balanced mineral content enhances the tea body and sweetness without masking flavor. Look for water with TDS (total dissolved solids) of <strong>30-80 ppm</strong> — this is the sweet spot for most Chinese teas. Mountain spring brands like Nongfu Spring (农夫山泉) are popular among Chinese tea drinkers for good reason.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl border border-zinc-200 p-6">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-zinc-900 mb-2 flex items-center gap-2"><span class="text-sky-600">&#128701;</span> Filtered Water (Home)</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-zinc-500 leading-relaxed"><strong>The practical choice.</strong> A good activated carbon filter removes chlorine, odors, and organic compounds while preserving the helpful minerals that give tea body. This is what most serious home brewers use day to day. Replace your filter on schedule — exhausted filters do more harm than good.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl border border-zinc-200 p-6">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-zinc-900 mb-2 flex items-center gap-2"><span class="text-amber-600">&#9888;</span> Distilled / RO Water</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-zinc-500 leading-relaxed"><strong>Too pure.</strong> Reverse osmosis and distilled water have almost zero mineral content. The result is a flat, thin, hollow-tasting tea — the water pulls flavor out of the leaves but has nothing to carry it. If you must use it, <strong>add a tiny pinch of mineral salt</strong> or blend 1:1 with spring water to restore body.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl border border-zinc-200 p-6">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-zinc-900 mb-2 flex items-center gap-2"><span class="text-amber-600">&#9888;</span> Tap &amp; Hard Water</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-zinc-500 leading-relaxed"><strong>Depends.</strong> If your tap water tastes good on its own, it will make decent tea. If it has a noticeable chlorine taste, filter it first. <strong>Hard water</strong> (high calcium/magnesium) is a problem — the minerals bind to tea compounds, suppressing aroma and creating a dull, flat brew with surface scum. Soften hard water before brewing.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="mt-8 max-w-5xl mx-auto text-center">
      <p class="text-sm text-zinc-400">The simplest rule: <strong>if the water tastes good on its own, it will make good tea.</strong> If you would not drink a glass of it plain, do not brew tea with it.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>



