<!-- ========== TEA CULTURE & CEREMONY ========== -->
<section id="culture" class="bg-leaf-950 py-20 text-white">
  <div class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-4">
    <div class="grid lg:grid-cols-2 gap-16 items-start">
      <div>
        <p class="text-leaf-300 text-sm font-mono uppercase tracking-[0.18em] mb-3">Tradition</p>
        <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight mb-6">
          Chinese Tea Culture &amp; Ceremony
        </h2>
        <p class="text-zinc-300 leading-relaxed mb-6">
          Tea in China is far more than a drink — it's woven into daily life, art, poetry, and philosophy. For over 4,000 years, tea has been a medium for hospitality, meditation, and social connection.
        </p>
        <div class="space-y-4 mt-8">
          <div class="flex gap-4">
            <div class="w-10 h-10 rounded-lg bg-leaf-800 flex items-center justify-center flex-shrink-0 text-leaf-300 mt-0.5">茶</div>
            <div>
              <h3 class="font-semibold text-white mb-1">Gongfu Tea Ceremony</h3>
              <p class="text-sm text-zinc-400">The Chinese approach to tea at its most refined. Using a small clay pot (often Yixing) or gaiwan, a high leaf-to-water ratio, and multiple short infusions (10-60 seconds each), gongfu cha reveals how a tea’s flavor evolves across 5-15+ steeps. Unlike the formal Japanese tea ceremony, gongfu cha is practiced casually at home — it’s less about ritual and <strong>more about the tea itself</strong>. The phrase means “tea made with skill,” and the focus is on extraction, temperature control, and appreciating the journey from first steep to last.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="flex gap-4">
            <div class="w-10 h-10 rounded-lg bg-leaf-800 flex items-center justify-center flex-shrink-0 text-leaf-300 mt-0.5">礼</div>
            <div>
              <h3 class="font-semibold text-white mb-1">Tea as Hospitality</h3>
              <p class="text-sm text-zinc-400">Serving tea to guests is a <strong>fundamental Chinese custom</strong> that predates recorded history. When you visit a Chinese home or office, tea appears before small talk begins — it’s a gesture of respect, warmth, and connection. The phrase "请喝茶" (please drink tea) is one of the most common expressions of welcome.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="flex gap-4">
            <div class="w-10 h-10 rounded-lg bg-leaf-800 flex items-center justify-center flex-shrink-0 text-leaf-300 mt-0.5">婚</div>
            <div>
              <h3 class="font-semibold text-white mb-1">Wedding Tea Ceremony</h3>
              <p class="text-sm text-zinc-400">The Chinese wedding tea ceremony is one of the <strong>most meaningful moments</strong> of the celebration. The bride and groom kneel before their parents and serve tea, formally transitioning from being “children” to “partners.” Parents often add auspicious ingredients — red dates (for early offspring), lotus seeds (for harmony), and longan (for sweetness). In return, parents offer red envelopes (hong bao) with gifts or money. This tradition appears across Chinese communities worldwide, from Beijing to San Francisco.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="flex gap-4">
            <div class="w-10 h-10 rounded-lg bg-leaf-800 flex items-center justify-center flex-shrink-0 text-leaf-300 mt-0.5">指</div>
            <div>
              <h3 class="font-semibold text-white mb-1">Finger Tapping (Kou Zhi Li / 扣指礼)</h3>
              <p class="text-sm text-zinc-400">When someone pours tea for you in China, you may see people <strong>tapping the table with their fingers</strong> instead of saying thank you. This is <strong>kou zhi li</strong> (扣指礼), a silent gesture of gratitude that dates back to the Qing Dynasty. <strong>Legend:</strong> Emperor Qianlong once traveled in disguise among commoners. When he poured tea for his servant, the servant couldn't kneel to express gratitude without revealing the emperor's identity. Instead, he bent his index and middle fingers and tapped the table — <strong>each bent finger representing a kneeling knee</strong>, a silent kowtow. The custom stuck and evolved. Today, the gesture varies by relationship: <strong>junior to senior</strong> — tap with curled knuckles (deeper respect); <strong>peers/equals</strong> — tap with two fingertips; <strong>senior to junior</strong> — tap once with a single finger. You'll see this everywhere from dim sum restaurants in Hong Kong to tea houses in Chengdu — a small gesture that carries centuries of history.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="flex gap-4">
            <div class="w-10 h-10 rounded-lg bg-leaf-800 flex items-center justify-center flex-shrink-0 text-leaf-300 mt-0.5">禅</div>
            <div>
              <h3 class="font-semibold text-white mb-1">Chan and Tea (Chan Cha Yi Wei / 禅茶一味)</h3>
              <p class="text-sm text-zinc-400">The saying <strong>chan cha yi wei</strong> (禅茶一味) — the oneness of Chan and tea — captures a thousand-year relationship between Buddhist meditation and tea drinking in China. During the Tang Dynasty, monks in Chan monasteries discovered that tea helped them <strong>stay alert during long hours of seated meditation</strong> without the agitation that other stimulants caused. The legendary founder of Chan Buddhism in China, Bodhidharma, is said to have torn off his eyelids to stay awake during meditation — and <strong>tea plants grew where they fell</strong>. Over centuries, tea became inseparable from monastic life: monasteries cultivated their own tea gardens, developed processing techniques, and used tea as both a meditation aid and a gesture of hospitality. The tea traditions of East Asia all trace back to this Chinese Chan-tea connection.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-leaf-900/50 rounded-2xl border border-leaf-800 p-8">
        <img src="/images/chinese-tea-culture.webp" alt="Chinese tea ceremony in a traditional setting" class="w-full rounded-xl mb-5" loading="lazy" width="800" height="533" />

        <h3 class="font-semibold text-lg text-white mb-4">Tea Culture Quick Facts</h3>
        <ul class="space-y-3 text-sm text-zinc-300">
          <li class="flex gap-3"><span class="text-leaf-400 font-mono">01</span> Tea originated in China around 2737 BCE</li>
          <li class="flex gap-3"><span class="text-leaf-400 font-mono">02</span> The "Tea Classic" (茶经) by Lu Yu was written in 760 CE</li>
          <li class="flex gap-3"><span class="text-leaf-400 font-mono">03</span> All six tea types come from the same plant — Camellia sinensis</li>
          <li class="flex gap-3"><span class="text-leaf-400 font-mono">04</span> China produces over 40% of the world's tea</li>
          <li class="flex gap-3"><span class="text-leaf-400 font-mono">05</span> Yixing clay teapots are prized for absorbing tea oils over decades</li>
          <li class="flex gap-3"><span class="text-leaf-400 font-mono">06</span> Aged pu-erh can cost as much as fine wine</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
<!-- ========== HEALTH BENEFITS ========== -->
<section id="health" class="bg-bone-50 py-20">
  <div class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-4">
    <div class="text-center mb-14">
      <p class="text-leaf-600 text-sm font-mono uppercase tracking-[0.18em] mb-3">Wellness</p>
      <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight text-zinc-900 mb-4">
        Health Benefits of Chinese Tea
      </h2>
      <p class="text-zinc-500 max-w-2xl mx-auto">
        For centuries, Chinese tea has been valued not just for flavor but for its effects on body and mind. Modern science is now catching up with tradition.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-6">
      <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl border border-zinc-200 p-6">
        <div class="w-10 h-10 rounded-xl bg-leaf-100 flex items-center justify-center mb-4 text-leaf-700 font-bold">A</div>
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-zinc-900 mb-2">Rich in Antioxidants</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-zinc-500 leading-relaxed">Chinese tea, especially green and white varieties, is packed with <strong>catechins</strong>, a class of polyphenol antioxidants. The most studied is <strong>EGCG</strong> (epigallocatechin gallate), which research suggests may help reduce oxidative stress and support cellular health. <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28864169/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-leaf-600 underline hover:text-leaf-700 text-xs">[source]</a> Green tea typically contains 30-40% catechins by dry weight — among the highest of any food.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl border border-zinc-200 p-6">
        <div class="w-10 h-10 rounded-xl bg-amber-100 flex items-center justify-center mb-4 text-amber-700 font-bold">Z</div>
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-zinc-900 mb-2">Calm Focus (L-Theanine)</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-zinc-500 leading-relaxed">Tea is the only natural source of <strong>L-theanine</strong>, an amino acid that promotes relaxed alertness. It increases alpha brain waves — the same state associated with meditation — <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18296328/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-leaf-600 underline hover:text-leaf-700 text-xs">[source]</a> — while <strong>counteracting caffeine jitters</strong>. This is why tea drinkers describe the energy as a calm, sustained focus rather than the spike and crash of coffee. Matcha and shade-grown teas are particularly high in L-theanine.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl border border-zinc-200 p-6">
        <div class="w-10 h-10 rounded-xl bg-red-100 flex items-center justify-center mb-4 text-red-700 font-bold">HT</div>
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-zinc-900 mb-2">Heart &amp; Metabolic Health</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-zinc-500 leading-relaxed">Regular tea consumption has been linked to improved cardiovascular markers in multiple large-scale studies. <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23780706/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-leaf-600 underline hover:text-leaf-700 text-xs">[source]</a> The combination of catechins and moderate caffeine may also support <strong>metabolic rate</strong>. Dark teas like Pu-erh have traditionally been consumed after heavy meals for their perceived digestive benefits — a custom now being studied for its effects on lipid metabolism.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="mt-10 bg-white rounded-2xl border border-zinc-200 p-6 md:p-8">
      <h3 class="font-semibold text-zinc-900 mb-4">Caffeine in Chinese Tea vs. Coffee</h3>
      <p class="text-sm text-zinc-500 leading-relaxed mb-4">An 8oz cup of coffee contains ~95mg of caffeine. Chinese tea varies widely <strong>by type and brewing method</strong>:</p>
      <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-3 text-sm">
        <div class="p-3 bg-bone-50 rounded-lg text-center"><span class="block text-xs text-zinc-400 mb-0.5">White Tea</span><span class="font-semibold text-zinc-800">15-25mg</span></div>
        <div class="p-3 bg-bone-50 rounded-lg text-center"><span class="block text-xs text-zinc-400 mb-0.5">Green Tea</span><span class="font-semibold text-zinc-800">25-35mg</span></div>
        <div class="p-3 bg-bone-50 rounded-lg text-center"><span class="block text-xs text-zinc-400 mb-0.5">Oolong</span><span class="font-semibold text-zinc-800">30-45mg</span></div>
        <div class="p-3 bg-bone-50 rounded-lg text-center"><span class="block text-xs text-zinc-400 mb-0.5">Black / Pu-erh</span><span class="font-semibold text-zinc-800">40-70mg</span></div>
      </div>
      <p class="text-xs text-zinc-400 mt-4">Multiple short gongfu infusions release caffeine more gradually than a single long Western steep. L-theanine in tea smooths out the caffeine curve, producing alert calm rather than a spike.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>



<!-- ========== TEA & FOOD PAIRING ========== -->
<section id="pairing" class="py-20 bg-bone-50">
  <div class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-4">
    <div class="text-center mb-14">
      <p class="text-amber-600 text-sm font-mono uppercase tracking-[0.18em] mb-3">Pairings</p>
      <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight text-zinc-900 mb-4">
        Chinese Tea &amp; Food Pairing
      </h2>
      <p class="text-zinc-500 max-w-2xl mx-auto">
        The right tea elevates a meal. Each type of Chinese tea has natural food companions that bring out its best qualities.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3 gap-6 max-w-6xl mx-auto">
      <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl border border-zinc-200 p-6 hover:shadow-md hover:border-emerald-200 transition-all group">
        <div class="flex items-start gap-4 mb-4">
          <div class="w-14 h-14 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 flex items-center justify-center text-3xl flex-shrink-0 group-hover:scale-110 transition-transform">&#127807;</div>
          <div>
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-lg text-zinc-900">Green Tea</h3>
            <p class="text-xs text-zinc-400 mt-0.5">Seafood · Salads · Light Dim Sum</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="text-sm text-zinc-500 leading-relaxed">The clean vegetal character of green tea cuts through delicate flavors without overwhelming them. Longjing with steamed fish is a Hangzhou classic. Biluochun alongside fresh spring greens makes each taste greener. Gunpowder green stands up to garlic and ginger in stir-fried dishes.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl border border-zinc-200 p-6 hover:shadow-md hover:border-amber-200 transition-all group">
        <div class="flex items-start gap-4 mb-4">
          <div class="w-14 h-14 rounded-xl bg-amber-50 flex items-center justify-center text-3xl flex-shrink-0 group-hover:scale-110 transition-transform">&#127793;</div>
          <div>
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-lg text-zinc-900">White Tea</h3>
            <p class="text-xs text-zinc-400 mt-0.5">Fresh Fruit · Mild Cheese · Pastries</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="text-sm text-zinc-500 leading-relaxed">White tea subtlety disappears next to strong flavors. Serve it with food as delicate as itself. The natural sweetness of Silver Needle complements honeydew melon or plain butter cookies. White Peony holds its own with soft cheeses and light salads.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl border border-zinc-200 p-6 hover:shadow-md hover:border-orange-200 transition-all group">
        <div class="flex items-start gap-4 mb-4">
          <div class="w-14 h-14 rounded-xl bg-orange-50 flex items-center justify-center text-3xl flex-shrink-0 group-hover:scale-110 transition-transform">&#127861;</div>
          <div>
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-lg text-zinc-900">Oolong</h3>
            <p class="text-xs text-zinc-400 mt-0.5">Dim Sum · Roasted Meats · Nuts</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="text-sm text-zinc-500 leading-relaxed">The most versatile pairing tea. Floral Tie Guan Yin complements har gow and siu mai beautifully. Roasted Da Hong Pao stands up to char siu and Peking duck. Dan Cong with roasted almonds is a Chaozhou ritual that has been practiced for centuries.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl border border-zinc-200 p-6 hover:shadow-md hover:border-red-200 transition-all group">
        <div class="flex items-start gap-4 mb-4">
          <div class="w-14 h-14 rounded-xl bg-red-50 flex items-center justify-center text-3xl flex-shrink-0 group-hover:scale-110 transition-transform">&#9749;</div>
          <div>
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-lg text-zinc-900">Black Tea</h3>
            <p class="text-xs text-zinc-400 mt-0.5">Breakfast · Chocolate · Baked Goods</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="text-sm text-zinc-500 leading-relaxed">Keemun is the natural breakfast tea — eggs, toast, pastries, all elevated. Dian Hong with dark chocolate is a revelation; the sweet potato notes mirror cocoa richness. Lapsang Souchong holds its own against barbecue and smoked meats like nothing else.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl border border-zinc-200 p-6 hover:shadow-md hover:border-stone-300 transition-all group">
        <div class="flex items-start gap-4 mb-4">
          <div class="w-14 h-14 rounded-xl bg-stone-100 flex items-center justify-center text-3xl flex-shrink-0 group-hover:scale-110 transition-transform">&#127858;</div>
          <div>
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-lg text-zinc-900">Dark Tea (Pu-erh)</h3>
            <p class="text-xs text-zinc-400 mt-0.5">Heavy Meats · Oily Dishes · Aged Cheese</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="text-sm text-zinc-500 leading-relaxed">Pu-erh is famous as a digestive aid after heavy meals. The earthy, fermented notes cut through grease and richness. Shou pu-erh after hot pot or BBQ is standard practice in Yunnan and Guangdong. Aged sheng with lamb stew is a winter ritual worth seeking out.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl border border-zinc-200 p-6 hover:shadow-md hover:border-yellow-200 transition-all group">
        <div class="flex items-start gap-4 mb-4">
          <div class="w-14 h-14 rounded-xl bg-yellow-50 flex items-center justify-center text-3xl flex-shrink-0 group-hover:scale-110 transition-transform">&#127856;</div>
          <div>
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-lg text-zinc-900">Yellow Tea</h3>
            <p class="text-xs text-zinc-400 mt-0.5">Light Soups · Tofu · Plain Congee</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="text-sm text-zinc-500 leading-relaxed">Yellow tea exceptional mellow character is easily overwhelmed. Serve it before or after a meal rather than during. If pairing, stick to the simplest foods — a bowl of plain rice porridge with Huoshan Huangya is a quiet, understated luxury.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
<!-- ========== TEAWARE ESSENTIALS ========== -->
<section class="py-20">
  <div class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-4">
    <div class="text-center mb-12">
      <p class="text-leaf-600 text-sm font-mono uppercase tracking-[0.18em] mb-3">Equipment</p>
      <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight text-zinc-900 mb-4">
        Teaware Essentials
      </h2>
      <p class="text-zinc-500 max-w-xl mx-auto">
        The right teaware transforms brewing from routine to ritual. Start with these fundamentals.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-6">
      <div class="text-center p-6 bg-white rounded-2xl border border-zinc-200 hover:border-leaf-200 transition-colors">
        <div class="w-16 h-16 mx-auto mb-4 rounded-xl bg-leaf-50 flex items-center justify-center text-3xl">🫖</div>
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-zinc-900 mb-1">Gaiwan</h3>
        <p class="text-xs text-zinc-500 leading-relaxed">The universal Chinese brewing vessel: bowl, lid, and saucer. Use for <strong>any tea type</strong> — the porcelain is neutral and won’t absorb flavors. The lid doubles as a strainer. Beginner-friendly and easy to clean. <span class="text-zinc-400 mt-1 block">Price: $8-20 for a basic porcelain gaiwan (100-150ml).</span></p>
      </div>
      <div class="text-center p-6 bg-white rounded-2xl border border-zinc-200 hover:border-leaf-200 transition-colors">
        <div class="w-16 h-16 mx-auto mb-4 rounded-xl bg-leaf-50 flex items-center justify-center text-3xl">🫗</div>
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-zinc-900 mb-1">Yixing Teapot</h3>
        <p class="text-xs text-zinc-500 leading-relaxed">Handcrafted from <strong>zisha (purple clay)</strong> found only in Yixing, Jiangsu. The unglazed, porous clay <strong>absorbs tea oils over time</strong>, enhancing flavor with each use. <strong>Dedicate one pot to one tea type</strong> (never mix green and pu-erh in the same Yixing pot). Not for beginners — start with a gaiwan first. <span class="text-zinc-400 mt-1 block">Price: $30-200+ depending on clay quality and craftsmanship.</span></p>
      </div>
      <div class="text-center p-6 bg-white rounded-2xl border border-zinc-200 hover:border-leaf-200 transition-colors">
        <div class="w-16 h-16 mx-auto mb-4 rounded-xl bg-leaf-50 flex items-center justify-center text-3xl">☕</div>
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-zinc-900 mb-1">Fairness Pitcher (Gong Dao Bei)</h3>
        <p class="text-xs text-zinc-500 leading-relaxed">The <strong>unsung hero of gongfu brewing</strong>. After steeping, pour tea from the gaiwan or teapot into the pitcher first — this <strong>equalizes the strength</strong> across all cups. Without it, the first pour is weak and the last pour is over-steeped. Glass pitchers let you admire the tea’s color. <span class="text-zinc-400 mt-1 block">Price: $10-30 for borosilicate glass.</span></p>
      </div>
      <div class="text-center p-6 bg-white rounded-2xl border border-zinc-200 hover:border-leaf-200 transition-colors">
        <div class="w-16 h-16 mx-auto mb-4 rounded-xl bg-leaf-50 flex items-center justify-center text-3xl">🧋</div>
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-zinc-900 mb-1">Aroma + Tasting Cups</h3>
        <p class="text-xs text-zinc-500 leading-relaxed">Gongfu brewing uses <strong>two cups per person</strong>: a tall, narrow <strong>aroma cup</strong> (wen xiang bei) to capture fragrance, and a wide <strong>tasting cup</strong> (pin ming bei) for sipping. Pour from aroma cup into tasting cup, then <strong>inhale the empty aroma cup</strong> — the fragrance lingers. For casual brewing, any small (30-60ml) porcelain cup works. <span class="text-zinc-400 mt-1 block">Price: $5-25 for a set of 4-6 cups.</span></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ========== BUYING GUIDE ========== -->
<section id="buying" class="py-20">
  <div class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-4">
    <div class="text-center mb-14">
      <p class="text-amber-600 text-sm font-mono uppercase tracking-[0.18em] mb-3">Practical Advice</p>
      <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight text-zinc-900 mb-4">
        How to Buy Authentic Chinese Tea
      </h2>
      <p class="text-zinc-500 max-w-2xl mx-auto">
        The Chinese tea market is vast and unregulated. Counterfeits are common. Here is how to shop with confidence.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 max-w-5xl mx-auto">
      <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl border border-zinc-200 p-6">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-zinc-900 mb-3 flex items-center gap-2"><span class="text-leaf-600">&#10003;</span> What to Look For</h3>
        <ul class="space-y-2 text-sm text-zinc-600">
          <li><strong>Harvest date:</strong> Freshness matters. Look for spring harvest dates for green, white, and oolong teas. Vendors who hide the harvest date are usually selling old stock.</li>
          <li><strong>Specific origin:</strong> Good tea names the mountain, village, or county. A tea from Shifeng is far more meaningful than just Zhejiang green tea.</li>
          <li><strong>Cultivar:</strong> Premium vendors specify the tea plant variety. Different cultivars of the same tea type can taste radically different.</li>
          <li><strong>Whole, intact leaves:</strong> Quality loose leaf tea should look like whole leaves, not dust. After brewing, the leaves should be intact and identifiable.</li>
          <li><strong>Dry aroma:</strong> Good tea smells good in the bag. A tea with no fragrance when dry will have no fragrance when brewed.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl border border-zinc-200 p-6">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-zinc-900 mb-3 flex items-center gap-2"><span class="text-amber-600">&#9888;</span> Red Flags to Avoid</h3>
        <ul class="space-y-2 text-sm text-zinc-600">
          <li><strong>No origin listed:</strong> If the tea just says Chinese Green Tea with no specific origin, assume it is the lowest-quality bulk commodity tea.</li>
          <li><strong>Suspiciously low prices:</strong> If a famous tea like Longjing is $5 per 100g, it is almost certainly fake. Premium Chinese teas cost more because skilled hand-processing is expensive.</li>
          <li><strong>Broken leaves or dust:</strong> Tea bags are almost always filled with the lowest grade of leaf fragments. Loose leaf with many broken pieces signals machine harvesting and rough handling.</li>
          <li><strong>Artificial fragrance:</strong> Some teas are sprayed with flavoring to mask low quality. Natural tea aroma evolves across infusions; artificial scents fade after the first steep.</li>
          <li><strong>Vague tasting notes:</strong> If a vendor cannot describe a tea beyond smooth or delicious, they either do not know the tea or it is not worth knowing.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="mt-10 bg-amber-50 rounded-2xl border border-amber-200 p-6 md:p-8 max-w-5xl mx-auto">
      <h3 class="font-semibold text-zinc-900 mb-3">Price Reality Check by Tea Type</h3>
      <p class="text-sm text-zinc-600 mb-4">These are realistic price ranges for good-quality tea from reputable sources. Prices below these ranges are red flags.</p>
      <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-3 gap-3 text-sm">
        <div class="p-3 bg-white rounded-lg"><span class="block text-xs text-zinc-400 mb-0.5">Green Tea</span><span class="font-semibold text-zinc-800">$8-30 / 100g</span><span class="block text-xs text-zinc-400">premium $40-100+</span></div>
        <div class="p-3 bg-white rounded-lg"><span class="block text-xs text-zinc-400 mb-0.5">White Tea</span><span class="font-semibold text-zinc-800">$10-25 / 100g</span><span class="block text-xs text-zinc-400">Silver Needle $50-100+</span></div>
        <div class="p-3 bg-white rounded-lg"><span class="block text-xs text-zinc-400 mb-0.5">Oolong</span><span class="font-semibold text-zinc-800">$8-30 / 100g</span><span class="block text-xs text-zinc-400">Wuyi yancha $30-300+</span></div>
        <div class="p-3 bg-white rounded-lg"><span class="block text-xs text-zinc-400 mb-0.5">Black Tea</span><span class="font-semibold text-zinc-800">$8-20 / 100g</span><span class="block text-xs text-zinc-400">premium $25-70</span></div>
        <div class="p-3 bg-white rounded-lg"><span class="block text-xs text-zinc-400 mb-0.5">Pu-erh (Shou)</span><span class="font-semibold text-zinc-800">$10-25 / 357g cake</span><span class="block text-xs text-zinc-400">aged $50-150+</span></div>
        <div class="p-3 bg-white rounded-lg"><span class="block text-xs text-zinc-400 mb-0.5">Pu-erh (Sheng)</span><span class="font-semibold text-zinc-800">$15-40 / 357g cake</span><span class="block text-xs text-zinc-400">famous mountain $100-500+</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="mt-6 max-w-5xl mx-auto">
      <p class="text-xs text-zinc-400 text-center">Each tea in our <a href="#famous" class="text-leaf-600 underline">Famous Teas table</a> above includes specific buying advice, quality checks, and common scams to watch for.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>




